# Section VI. Control Phase (15 of 100 scored; 7 per Tigertest test)
CAT = "cssgbControl"; SEG = "CTL"
Q = [
("What is the main objective of statistical process control?",
 "To detect special causes early", ["To sort good parts from bad", "To set the specification limits", "To remove all variation"],
 "SPC monitors a process over time so special cause variation is spotted and acted on before it produces defects. Some common cause variation always remains."),
("Which describes common cause variation?",
 "Built-in process noise", ["A sudden tool breakage", "A new untrained operator", "A bad batch of material"],
 "Common causes are the many small sources always present in a stable process. Reducing them needs a change to the process itself, usually by management."),
("A point falls outside the upper control limit after a material change. What does this most likely signal?",
 "A special cause", ["Common cause noise", "A need for wider limits", "A specification change"],
 "A point beyond a 3 sigma limit is unlikely from common causes alone, so the team looks for an assignable cause, here probably the new material."),
("An operator adjusts the machine after every point that is not exactly on target. What is the likely result?",
 "More variation, not less", ["Less variation over time", "A perfectly centered process", "No change in variation"],
 "Adjusting a stable process in response to common cause noise is tampering (over-adjustment), which Deming's funnel experiment shows increases variation."),
("Which pattern on a control chart signals a special cause even though every point is inside the limits?",
 "A long run on one side of the center", ["Points spread randomly about the center", "About two thirds within one sigma", "No points beyond the limits"],
 "Run rules flag non-random patterns: a long run on one side of the center line, a steady trend, or repeated points near a limit, as well as points beyond the limits."),
("What is the aim of rational subgrouping?",
 "Only common causes within subgroups", ["Every subgroup has equal means", "Subgroups mix shifts on purpose", "Subgroups are as large as possible"],
 "Rational subgroups are chosen, often consecutive parts made close together in time, so that within-subgroup variation reflects only common causes and shifts appear between subgroups."),
("A team forms each subgroup from one part per shift over three shifts. What is the risk?",
 "Range picks up shift effects", ["The chart cannot be drawn", "The limits become too narrow", "No data can be used at all"],
 "Mixing shifts inside a subgroup puts between-shift variation into the range, so the limits widen and real shift-to-shift changes go undetected."),
("Why are subgroups usually small, such as 4 or 5 consecutive parts?",
 "To capture short-term variation", ["To save money on gauges", "To force the data to normal", "To fill the chart more quickly"],
 "Small subgroups taken close together keep within-subgroup variation to short-term common causes, which makes the chart sensitive to shifts between subgroups."),
("Which chart pair suits continuous data collected in subgroups of 5?",
 "X-bar and R", ["p and np", "c and u", "Individuals and moving range"],
 "X-bar and R charts track the subgroup average and range for variable data in small subgroups. ImR is for one reading at a time."),
("Which chart pair is preferred for variable data in subgroups of 12 or more?",
 "X-bar and s", ["X-bar and R", "Individuals and moving range", "np and p"],
 "With larger subgroups the standard deviation estimates spread more efficiently than the range, so X-bar and s charts are used."),
("An X-bar and R chart uses subgroups of 5, a grand mean of 50.0 and an average range of 4.0. With A2 = 0.577, what is the upper control limit for X-bar?",
 "52.31", ["54.00", "51.15", "58.46"],
 "UCL = grand mean + A2 x R-bar = 50.0 + 0.577 x 4.0 = 52.31. 54.00 adds the full average range."),
("For the same chart (average range 4.0, n = 5), what is the R chart's upper control limit with D4 = 2.114?",
 "8.46", ["6.31", "4.00", "2.31"],
 "UCL for R = D4 x R-bar = 2.114 x 4.0 = 8.46. For n of 6 or fewer, D3 is 0, so the lower limit is 0."),
("An individuals chart has a mean of 120 and an average moving range of 6. Using 2.66 x MR-bar, what is the upper control limit?",
 "About 136.0", ["About 126.0", "About 138.0", "About 139.6"],
 "UCL = X-bar + 2.66 x MR-bar = 120 + 15.96, about 136.0. Adding 3 x 6 = 18 uses the moving range as if it were sigma."),
("When is an individuals and moving range (ImR) chart the right choice?",
 "One reading per period", ["Defect counts per unit", "Subgroups of 5 parts", "Percent defective in lots"],
 "ImR charts suit data that come one value at a time, such as a daily yield or a batch property, where subgrouping is not practical."),
("A plant inspects lots of different sizes and records the fraction defective in each. Which chart fits?",
 "p chart", ["np chart", "c chart", "X-bar and R chart"],
 "The p chart tracks proportion defective and allows the sample size to vary, with limits recalculated for each n. The np chart needs a constant sample size."),
("Inspectors check a fixed 100 invoices a day and count how many have an error. Which chart fits best?",
 "np chart", ["u chart", "c chart", "Individuals chart"],
 "With a constant sample size and a count of defective items, the np chart plots the number defective directly."),
("A p chart has p-bar = 0.06 and a constant sample size of 200. About where is the upper control limit?",
 "0.110", ["0.077", "0.060", "0.130"],
 "UCL = p-bar + 3 x square root of (p-bar x (1 - p-bar) / n) = 0.06 + 3 x 0.0168, about 0.110. One sigma instead of three gives 0.077."),
("A c chart of flaws per bolt of fabric has an average of 9 flaws. What is its upper control limit?",
 "18", ["12", "27", "15"],
 "For a c chart, sigma is the square root of c-bar, here 3, so UCL = 9 + 3 x 3 = 18. The lower limit is 9 - 9 = 0."),
("Panels of different sizes are inspected for flaws. Which chart tracks flaws per unit of area?",
 "u chart", ["c chart", "np chart", "R chart"],
 "The u chart plots defects per unit and handles a changing area of opportunity. The c chart needs the same area of opportunity each time."),
("Which item belongs in a control plan?",
 "A reaction plan", ["The project charter", "The brainstorm list", "The meeting schedule"],
 "A control plan lists each characteristic, its specification, measurement method, sample size and frequency, the control method and what to do when it goes out of control."),
("Who should own the control plan after the project closes?",
 "The process owner", ["The Green Belt", "The external auditor", "The project sponsor's assistant"],
 "The process owner runs the process day to day, so ownership of the control plan passes to them at handoff to sustain the gains."),
("Why is document control important for sustaining an improvement?",
 "Only current versions are used", ["It removes the need for training", "It replaces the control chart", "It keeps documents secret"],
 "Document control makes sure only approved, current procedures are in use and old versions are withdrawn, so people do not drift back to the old method."),
("Which belongs in a training plan for a new standard method?",
 "Who, how and by when", ["The project's savings target", "The fishbone categories", "The supplier's price list"],
 "A training plan sets out the audience, content, method, schedule and how competence will be checked, so the new way is learned and kept."),
("A customer sends its own auditor to check a supplier's process. What type of audit is this?",
 "Second-party", ["First-party", "Third-party", "Internal"],
 "First-party audits are internal, second-party audits are by a customer of its supplier, and third-party audits are by an independent body, such as a registrar."),
("An independent registrar audits a company against ISO 9001. What type of audit is this?",
 "Third-party", ["First-party", "Second-party", "Process"],
 "A third-party audit is carried out by an organization independent of both supplier and customer, usually for certification."),
("In PDCA, what happens in the check step?",
 "Results are compared to plan", ["The change is made permanent", "The problem is first defined", "Data collection begins"],
 "Plan the change, do it on a small scale, check the results against what was expected, then act to adopt, adapt or abandon it."),
("Which TPM practice uses data such as vibration readings to service equipment before it fails?",
 "Predictive maintenance", ["Breakdown maintenance", "Corrective maintenance", "Run-to-failure repair"],
 "Predictive maintenance monitors condition to forecast when a failure is likely and services the equipment just in time, as part of total productive maintenance."),
("A machine stops itself and lights an andon when it detects a defect. Which lean concept is this?",
 "Jidoka", ["Heijunka", "Kanban", "Takt time"],
 "Jidoka (automation with a human touch) stops the process at the first sign of a problem; the andon light is the visual signal that calls for help."),
]
